{"name":"Barn Door","hex":"#8e5959","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"barn door","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The large door of a barn.","Something large enough that a miss ought to be impossible."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply or adjust the barn doors as part of lighting."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barn_door"}],"entries":[{"title":"Barn","blocks":[{"text":"A barn is an agricultural building usually on farms and used for various purposes. In North America, a barn refers to structures that house livestock, including cattle and horses, as well as equipment and fodder, and often grain. As a result, the term barn is often qualified e.g. tobacco barn, dairy barn, cow house, sheep barn, potato barn. In the British Isles and in Europe, the term barn is restricted mainly to storage structures for unthreshed cereals and fodder, the terms byre, shippon or shippen being applied to cow shelters, whereas horses are kept in buildings known as stables. In mainland Europe, however, barns were often part of integrated structures known as byre-dwellings. In addition, barns may be used for equipment storage, as a covered workplace, and for activities such as threshing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg/330px-Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barn"},{"title":"Door","blocks":[{"text":"A door is a hinged or otherwise movable barrier that allows ingress (entry) into and egress (exit) from an enclosure. The created opening in the wall is a doorway or portal. A door's essential and primary purpose is to provide security by controlling access to the doorway (portal). Conventionally, it is a panel that fits into the doorway of a building, room, or vehicle. Doors are generally made of a material suited to the door's task. They are commonly attached by hinges, but can move by other means, such as slides or counterbalancing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/L-door.png/330px-L-door.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Door"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg/500px-Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg/1280px-Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg/500px-Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Timber framed with siding of vertical boards was typical in early New England. The traditional color is the result of iron oxide stain applied to protect the wood from UV damage.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/TTCdairybarn.jpg/330px-TTCdairybarn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/TTCdairybarn.jpg/330px-TTCdairybarn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Texas Technological College Dairy Barn in Lubbock, Texas, U.S., was used as a teaching facility until 1967.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATTCdairybarn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%93%D1%83%D0%BC%D0%BD%D0%BE_1894.jpg/500px-%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%93%D1%83%D0%BC%D0%BD%D0%BE_1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&u